{"name":"Samphire Green","hex":"#4db560","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"samphire","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of several salt-tolerant plants, some edible:\nPlants of genus Salicornia (syn. Salicornia)\nMarsh samphire, glasswort, pickleweed (Salicornia europaea), a plant once burned to produce ash used to make soda glass.\nRock samphire (Crithmum maritimum).\nGolden samphire (Limbarda crithmoides, syn. Inula crithmoides).\ntree seaside tansy (Borrichia arborescens) of the West Indies.\nPlant of the species Gomphrena vermicularis\nPlants of genus Tecticornia","Plants of genus Salicornia (syn. Salicornia)\nMarsh samphire, glasswort, pickleweed (Salicornia europaea), a plant once burned to produce ash used to make soda glass."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/samphire"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Samphire","blocks":[{"text":"Samphire is a name given to a number of succulent salt-tolerant plants (halophytes) that tend to be associated with water bodies."},{"items":["Rock samphire is a coastal species with white flowers that grows in Ireland, the United Kingdom and the Isle of Man. This is probably the species mentioned by Shakespeare in King Lear.","Golden samphire is a coastal species with yellow flowers that grows across Eurasia.","Several species in the genus Salicornia, known as \"marsh samphire\" in Britain.","Blutaparon vermiculare, Central America, southeastern North America","Tecticornia, Australia","Sarcocornia, cosmopolitan"]}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Norfolk_Samphire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samphire","wikidata":"Q7410111"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Image_of_fresh_samphire_for_sale_at_Swansea_Market.jpg/500px-Image_of_fresh_samphire_for_sale_at_Swansea_Market.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Image_of_fresh_samphire_for_sale_at_Swansea_Market.jpg/1280px-Image_of_fresh_samphire_for_sale_at_Swansea_Market.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Image_of_fresh_samphire_for_sale_at_Swansea_Market.jpg/5